re value. But the features are current and past values. Wait, but in the code, the target is the future value. So when the model is trained, the features are up to the current day, and the target is the next day's value. That's a time series prediction setup. But the problem is whether the features include future data. \\\\n\\\\nWait, the code creates \\\\\\\"lag1\\\\\\\" as shift(1), which is past data. The \\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\" column is the current value. So the features are current and past values. The target is the next day's value. So the model is predicting the next day's value based on current and past values. That's a standard time series setup. But the question is about using future data in the features. \\\\n\\\\nWait, the code's \\\\\\\"lag1\\\\\\\" is shift(1), which is past. The \\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\" is the current value. So the features are current and past. The target is the future. So the features don't include future data. Therefore, there's no data leakage here. \\\\n\\\\nBut wait, the user's question is about negative shifts in the features. The code's \\\\\\\"lag1\\\\\\\" is shift(1), which is positive. The target is shift(-1), which is future. But the target is the label, not a feature. The features are X, which are [\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"lag1\\\\\\\"], which are current and past. So no negative shifts in the features. Therefore, the code doesn't use future data in the features. \\\\n\\\\nSo the answer should be NO. The code is correct in that the features don't include future data. The target is the future value, which is the correct setup for time series prediction. The features are based on past and current data. So no data leakage here.\\\\n\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pattern_id\\\": \\\"ml-005\\\",\\n        \\\"detected\\\": \\\"no\\\",\\n        \\\"confidence\\\": 0.95,\\n        \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"train_test_split uses shuffle=False and no cross-validation splitter is employed\\\",\\n        \\\"thinking\\\": \